# Bind-parameter index limit — a statement may name parameters up to $65535 (MAX_PARAM_INDEX,
# PostgreSQL's wire-protocol limit on the parameters of one statement); a larger index is 42601
# at the lexer (CLAUDE.md §13; spec/design/api.md §5, grammar.md §5). The parameter table a
# statement allocates is sized by its LARGEST index, so without the bound `SELECT $4294967295`
# allocated four billion slots and killed the process (a fatal out-of-memory, not an engine
# error) — the §13 resource gate the cost ceiling cannot reach, since the allocation precedes
# metering. The bound is a fixed, cross-core-identical constant, like the nesting-depth limit:
# the same statement is accepted or rejected identically in Rust / Go / TS. jed-specific in its
# trigger (PostgreSQL's parser accepts any index and its Bind message carries at most 65535), so
# not oracle-checked.
# requires: query.select_no_from, resource.param_index_limit

# $65535 is the highest index the lexer accepts: it is lexed, and the statement then fails only
# because the parameter has no type context (42P18) — a resolve-time error, past the lexer.
statement error 42P18
SELECT $65535

# One past the maximum is rejected at the lexer with 42601, whatever follows.
statement error 42601
SELECT $65536

# A huge index — the one that exhausted memory — is the same 42601.
statement error 42601
SELECT $4294967295

# An index past any machine integer is rejected the same way, never parsed as a number first.
statement error 42601
SELECT $99999999999999999999999

# The other lexer rules are unchanged: $0 and a leading zero are 42601.
statement error 42601
SELECT $0

statement error 42601
SELECT $01
